ession, we get:\n\n\n</partial_draft>","role":"user"},{"content":"You may re-analyze the problem and rewrite the solution from the beginning.","role":"user"}],"rendered_sha256":"2b4a6b459c29e20feb7a9b1376aea9664375c033bb8d05d42076bd8ceea876ce"},"seconds":57.43119812011719,"seed":396339230524910996,"stop":"eos","text":"To find \\(\\mathbf{c} \\times (3 \\mathbf{a} - 2 \\mathbf{b})\\), we will use the distributive property of the cross product. The distributive property tells us that for any vectors \\(\\mathbf{u}, \\mathbf{v}, \\mathbf{w}\\) and scalars \\(m, n\\):\n\n\\[\n\\mathbf{u} \\times (m\\mathbf{v} + n\\mathbf{w}) = m(\\mathbf{u} \\times \\mathbf{v}) + n(\\mathbf{u} \\times \\mathbf{w}).\n\\]\n\nApplying this property to our specific problem, we have:\n\n\\[\n\\mathbf{c} \\times (3 \\mathbf{a} - 2 \\mathbf{b}) = 3 (\\mathbf{c} \\times \\mathbf{a}) - 2 (\\mathbf{c} \\times \\mathbf{b}).\n\\]\n\nWe are given:\n\\[\n\\mathbf{a} \\times \\mathbf{c} = \\begin{pmatrix} 4 \\\\ 7 \\\\ 2 \\end{pmatrix}.\n\\]\n\nSince the cross product is anti-commutative (i.e., \\(\\mathbf{a} \\times \\mathbf{c} = - (\\mathbf{c} \\times \\mathbf{a})\\)), we get:\n\n\\[\n\\mathbf{c} \\times \\mathbf{a} = -\\begin{pmatrix} 4 \\\\ 7 \\\\ 2 \\end{pmatrix} = \\begin{pmatrix} -4 \\\\ -7 \\\\ -2 \\end{pmatrix}.\n\\]\n\nWe are also given:\n\\[\n\\mathbf{b} \\times \\mathbf{c} = \\begin{pmatrix} 1 \\\\ -7 \\\\ 18 \\end{pmatrix}.\n\\]\n\nSince \\(\\mathbf{c} \\times \\mathbf{b} = - (\\mathbf{b} \\times \\mathbf{c})\\), we get:\n\n\\[\n\\mathbf{c} \\times \\mathbf{b} = -\\begin{pmatrix} 1 \\\\ -7 \\\\ 18 \\end{pmatrix} = \\begin{pmatrix} -1 \\\\ 7 \\\\ -18 \\end{pmatrix}.\n\\]\n\nNow, substituting \\(\\mathbf{c} \\times \\mathbf{a}\\) and \\(\\mathbf{c} \\times \\mathbf{b}\\) into the expression \\(3 (\\mathbf{c} \\times \\mathbf{a}) - 2 (\\mathbf{c} \\times \\mathbf{b})\\), we get:\n\n\\[\n3 \\begin{pmatrix} -4 \\\\ -7 \\\\ -2 \\end{pmatrix} - 2 \\begin{pmatrix} -1 \\\\ 7 \\\\ -18 \\end{pmatrix}.\n\\]\n\nCarrying out the scalar multiplications, we get:\n\n\\[\n3 \\begin{pmatrix} -4 \\\\ -7 \\\\ -2 \\end{pmatrix} = \\begin{pmatrix} -12 \\\\ -21 \\\\ -6 \\end{pmatrix},\n\\]\n\nand\n\n\\[\n-2 \\begin{pmatrix} -1 \\\\ 7 \\\\ -18 \\end{pmatrix} = \\begin{pmatrix} 2 \\\\ -14 \\\\ 36 \\end{pmatrix}.\n\\]\n\nAdding these two resultant vectors together, we get:\n\n\\[\n\\begin{pmatrix} -12 \\\\ -21 \\\\ -6 \\end{pmatrix} + \\begin{pmatrix} 2 \\\\ -14 \\\\ 36 \\end{pmatrix} = \\begin{pmatrix} -10 \\\\ -35 \\\\ 30 \\end{pmatrix}.\n\\]\n\nThus, the final answer is:\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\begin{pmatrix} -10 \\\\ -35 \\\\ 30 \\end{pmatrix}}.\n\\]"},"grade":{"answer":"\\boxed{\\begin{pmatrix} -10 \\\\ -35 \\\\ 30 \\end{pmatrix}}","correct":true,"format":"boxed","parseable":true,"retains_student_answer":false},"graded_text":"To find \\(\\mathbf{c} \\times (3 \\mathbf{a} - 2 \\mathbf{b})\\), we will use the distributive property of the cross product. The distributive property tells us that for any vectors \\(\\mathbf{u}, \\mathbf{v}, \\mathbf{w}\\) and scalars \\(m, n\\):\n\n\\[\n\\mathbf{u} \\times (m\\mathbf{v} + n\\mathbf{w}) = m(\\mathbf{u} \\times \\mathbf{v}) + n(\\mathbf{u} \\times \\mathbf{w}).\n\\]\n\nApplying this property to our specific problem, we have:\n\n\\[\n\\mathbf{c} \\times (3 \\mathbf{a} - 2 \\mathbf{b}) = 3 (\\mathbf{c} \\times \\mathbf{a}) - 2 (\\mathbf{c} \\times \\mathbf{b}).\n\\]\n\nWe are given:\n\\[\n\\mathbf{a} \\times \\mathbf{c} = \\begin{pmatrix} 4 \\\\ 7 \\\\ 2 \\end{pmatrix}.\n\\]\n\nSince the cross product is anti-commutative (i.e., \\(\\mathbf{a} \\times \\mathbf{c} = - (\\mathbf{c} \\times \\mathbf{a})\\)), we get:\n\n\\[\n\\mathbf{c} \\times \\mathbf{a} = -\\begin{pmatrix} 4 \\\\ 7 \\\\ 2 \\end{pmatrix} = \\begin{pmatrix} -4 \\\\ -7 \\\\ -2 \\end{pmatrix}.\n\\]\n\nWe are also given:\n\\[\n\\mathbf{b} \\times \\mathbf{c} = \\begin{pmatrix} 1 \\\\ -7 \\\\ 18 \\end{pmatrix}.\n\\]\n\nSince \\(\\mathbf{c} \\times \\mathbf{b} = - (\\mathbf{b} \\times \\mathbf{c})\\), we get:\n\n\\[\n\\mathbf{c} \\times \\mathbf{b} = -\\begin{pmatrix} 1 \\\\ -7 \\\\ 18 \\end{pmatrix} = \\begin{pmatrix} -1 \\\\ 7 \\\\ -18 \\end{pmatrix}.\n\\]\n\nNow, substituting \\(\\mathbf{c} \\times \\mathbf{a}\\) and \\(\\mathbf{c} \\times \\mathbf{b}\\) into the expression \\(3 (\\mathbf{c} \\times \\mathbf{a}) - 2 (\\mathbf{c} \\times \\mathbf{b})\\), we get:\n\n\\[\n3 \\begin{pmatrix} -4 \\\\ -7 \\\\ -2 \\end{pmatrix} - 2 \\begin{pmatrix} -1 \\\\ 7 \\\\ -18 \\end{pmatrix}.\n\\]\n\nCarrying out the scalar multiplications, we get:\n\n\\[\n3 \\begin{pmatrix} -4 \\\\ -7 \\\\ -2 \\end{pmatrix} = \\begin{pmatrix} -12 \\\\ -21 \\\\ -6 \\end{pmatrix},\n\\]\n\nand\n\n\\[\n-2 \\begin{pmatrix} -1 \\\\ 7 \\\\ -18 \\end{pmatrix} = \\begin{pmatrix} 2 \\\\ -14 \\\\ 36 \\end{pmatrix}.\n\\]\n\nAdding these two resultant vectors together, we get:\n\n\\[\n\\begin{pmatrix} -12 \\\\ -21 \\\\ -6 \\end{pmatrix} + \\begin{pmatrix} 2 \\\\ -14 \\\\ 36 \\end{pmatrix} = \\begin{pmatrix} -10 \\\\ -35 \\\\ 30 \\end{pmatrix}.\n\\]\n\nThus, the final answer is:\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\begin{pmatrix} -10 \\\\ -35 \\\\ 30 \\end{pmatrix}}.\n\\]","id":"precalculus:429"}
